\subsection{Paired closures with dispersed charging facilities}

Twelve further base networks isolate the effect of closure duration. Six use a corridor ladder with lateral loops; six connect two regional loops through two links. Each topology has two networks at each of the existing S, M and L scales. Three dispersed nodes provide unit-capacity chargers, and final missions originate at multiple nodes. Assignments, mission chains, suffix holds and the phase-based aircraft model remain fixed. Successive mission endpoints are continuous in this extension, without additional positioning flights or intermediate charging stops.

Each network is evaluated under 10- and 35-minute closures of the same directed corridor. All other inputs, including initial SOC, incumbent charging and demand, are identical within a pair. Four separate development networks established the common construction rules. Formal generation retains every structurally valid network with a feasible incumbent, irrespective of subsequent method performance or action changes. The resulting 24 tasks all have certified six-tier references. Figure~\ref{fig:new_pairs} shows every pair, including those that retain waiting under the longer closure.


\subsection{Evaluation criteria}

\subsection{Closure duration and route--energy coordination}
\label{sec:new_structure}
Extending the corridor closure from ten to 35 minutes changes waiting into rerouting in seven of the twelve new networks; four pairs also change charging decisions. The other five pairs retain waiting. Both conditions preserve all 244 missions, including 87 urgent missions, but urgent completions on time fall from 78 to 72. The longer closure produces eleven rerouted mission occurrences and increases charging from 36 to 44 blocks. The change in recovery cost therefore remains visible even when service counts are unchanged.

