Restricted optimization in Regions S02 and Ladder L02 establishes that retaining all incumbent service routes or all incumbent charging counts prevents full service under the long closure. Both action classes must therefore change in these two cases. The twelve pairs preserve the order of all ten comparable pairs of cross-UAV users at shared chargers; the observed transition concerns route energy and chain timing rather than a reversal of charging order.


\subsection{Recovery priorities and the decision to reroute}
\label{sec:network_preferences}
Prioritizing weighted tardiness ahead of plan-change cost changes the preferred response to disruption. Across the 36 network tasks, seventeen move from waiting to rerouting, and the total number of service-route changes increases from one to 23. Urgent missions completed on time increase from 245 to 263 out of 294, while urgent and total service remain at 294 and 704 missions. Weighted tardiness falls from 4450 to 2788.49 weighted minutes. Thirteen tasks change charging decisions and ten reverse at least one shared-resource order. On the thirty tasks with the same served mission identities, flight energy increases by 0.78\%.

Restricted optimization separates the role of routing from that of charging. In six representative tasks, retaining incumbent routes still permits the optimal service counts but increases weighted tardiness. Retaining incumbent charging prevents those service counts on the three SOC-disrupted tasks, whereas the three corridor-only tasks attain the preferred objective vector with unchanged charging. Thus, route changes improve punctuality in these cases, while charging changes can determine whether service is recoverable. The original preference strongly favors schedule stability: its twenty-point route penalty equals the direct penalty for a hundred-minute departure shift on one mission, although chain and resource constraints govern the network-wide trade-off.

\subsection{Core diagnostics: local scope and capacity effects}

\subsection{Charging before an energy-intensive detour}

Regions S02 has three UAVs and nine missions. A closure of V02$\rightarrow$V01 begins at minute ten and ends at minute twenty or 45. Under the short closure, urgent mission M009 uses its incumbent route at minute 23. Under the long closure, reopening occurs after the departure horizon at minute 35, so the reference reroutes M009 and M002 while retaining all service.

M009's detour increases flight energy from 15.212 to 26.461 Wh. U03 reaches the preceding mission's destination with 38.805 Wh. Executing the detour without additional charging would leave 12.344 Wh; one five-minute block would leave 19.678 Wh, still below the required 19.980-Wh reserve. The reference adds two blocks after M008, departs at minute 33 and arrives at minute 36.161 with a 7.031-Wh reserve margin. The mission is served but finishes 6.347 minutes after its due time (Figure~\ref{fig:new_route_energy}).

The longer closure thus converts waiting into charging before a more energy-intensive route. The required charge consumes departure slack and transfers the disruption into urgent tardiness. Arrival after the departure horizon remains permissible under the operating contract.

